Note: Reason why interviewers ask this question. State file is the very important concept of Terraform.
If you know how to manage state file Terraform, then you have solved the major problem with Terraform.

Let me first explain what is state file management. Basically in Terraform, state file is the heart of Terraform.
State file has the information of resources that you created with Terraform. It has information of dependencies of the resource that you created.
It also has the information of last state of your infrastructure. That means, Whatever resources you create with Terraform and AWS state file has the resource IDs.
It has the dependencies and one resource is dependent on other resource. So during the deletion of infrastructure Terraform uses this dependency graph.
And Terraform also has the last or the previous state of your current state of your infrastructure. 

Terraform refers to this state file when you ask to create a new resource, or even when you ask to create or update an existing resource.
Let's say you have created an EC2 instance. Now you want to update some configuration of the EC2 instance. 
Terraform looks at the last state of this EC2 instance, whatever configuration you are asking it to update.
It will just update that configuration on EC2 rather than creating a new EC2 instance. It will just work on the configuration update.

How Terraform looks at the state file.
It will try to see what is the EC2 instance resource ID. It will check if the resource ID exists. It will check if there is any dependency, 
what is the last state and then it will update the EC2.

Now the biggest problem is because state file has all the information of your infrastructure. You cannot place it anywhere.
Let's say if you have the state file on your local machine, then other DevOps engineer when tries to execute the HCL files on the git repository, 
Terraform installed on that developer or DevOps engineers machine cannot locate the state file because it is on X developer's personal laptop.
So this is the reason why you need this state file in a centralized location so that everyone can access the state file, and anyone who runs Terraform state 
file is updated. Irrespective if I'm running on my machine, if I'm running on a common server, if I'm running on someone else's machine, anywhere.
Any DevOps engineer runs Terraform state file will be updated if it is stored in a centralized location.

Now what is that centralized location? Can you store state file in git along with the headsail files?
Obviously no, because state file has some sensitive information such as your resource IDs, IP address of your instances, 
the information of the dependencies, sometimes some certificates. So you cannot store the state file in a git repository because it becomes public, 
or at least it becomes public in your organization and its a bad choice. You cannot store it on your local machine. You cannot store it on a git repository. 
So where else can you store? So this is where Terraform supports something called as remote backend.
So remote backend is a centralized either a version control system or a centralized storage that is supported by Terraform such as S3 bucket on AWS, 
blob storage on Azure or Google GCS on Google Cloud. So you can use any of these things as remote backend.
So you just need to define in one of your ACL file, or you create ACL file called backend.tf. And within this ACL file 
you will provide the configuration of S3 bucket, blob or GCS.

Also, these remote backends are capable enough to ensure if someone is running this CL file state file is logged on their name.
Because you cannot afford multiple people running the same Terraform files at once. Because let's say one of the developer is trying to update EC2 instance, 
another developer is also parallelly trying to update the same EC2 instance. Then it will be a deadlock, right?

It will be a situation where Terraform does not understand which instruction to execute. So these backends are also capable of locking the state file.
So if let's say I am running the Terraform files state file in S3 bucket blob or GCS will be logged with my name.
And only once my Terraform execution is done, the lock is released and then other person will be able to run the terraform files 
and log is established on their name. So this entire process is called as state file management.

Response in Interview:
In a nutshell, state file management is a process of deciding where to store the state file and how to connect your files or terraform files to the state file.
In our organization, we store statefile in remote backend. Terraform supports various remote backends such as S3, Azure Blob, GCS console, and multiple others.
But because we are using AWS in our current organization, we are using S3 bucket to store the state file. And we have also enabled versioning on the S3 bucket.
We have enabled locking on S3 bucket. And also we have enabled security. So this way we are also taking care of versioning locking and security of the state file.
